     "type": "IMPORTANT",
     "title": null,
     "text": "**Definition: Set of Linearly Independent Vectors [Slide 156, 157]**  \nA set of vectors $\\{\\vec{v}_1, \\vec{v}_2, \\dots, \\vec{v}_k\\}$ is said to be **Linearly Independent** if and only if **ALL** vectors are independent of each other.  \nEquivalently, the vector equation:\n$$\\lambda_1 \\vec{v}_1 + \\lambda_2 \\vec{v}_2 + \\dots + \\lambda_k \\vec{v}_k = \\vec{0}$$\nhas **ONLY the trivial solution**:\n$$\\lambda_1 = \\lambda_2 = \\dots = \\lambda_k = 0$$\nContrapositively: If $\\sum_{i=1}^k \\vert \\lambda_i \\vert \\neq 0$, then $\\sum_{i=1}^k \\lambda_i \\vec{v}_i \\neq \\vec{0}$."
